<!DOCTYPE html><html><head><title>IRC channel logs</title><style>html {
  background: #fdfdfd;
}

h1 {
  font-weight: 300;
}

h2 {
  font-weight: 200;
}

h3 {
  padding: .5em 0;
  border-top: 3px dotted #ddd;
  margin-bottom: 0;
}

form {
  width: 400px;
  display: flex;
}

input {
  width: 100%;
  display: flex;
  border-radius: .25em 0 0 .25em;
  border: 1px solid #aaa;
  border-right: 0;
  padding: 0.5em;
}

button {
  display: flex;
  border-radius: 0 .25em .25em 0;
  background-color: #007bff;
  border: 1px solid #007bff;
  padding: .5em;
  cursor: pointer;
  color: white;
}

button:hover {
  background-color: #0069d9;
  border-color: #0062cc;
}

a {
  color: #007bff;
  text-decoration: none;
}

a:hover {
  text-decoration: underline;
}

h4 {
  margin-bottom: .5em;
}

table td {
  padding: 0.75em;
}

table tr:hover {
  background: #eee;
}

.year {
  display: table;
}

.month {
  display: table-cell;
  padding-right: 1em;
}

ul {
  margin: 0;
  padding: 0;
  list-style: none;
}

.nick {
  padding-right: 0.6rem;
  font-weight: bold;
  text-align: right;
  width: 13rem;
  display: table-cell;
}

.nick a {
  color: inherit;
  text-decoration: none;
}

.message {
  display: table-cell;
  padding-left: 0.6rem;
  border-left: 2px solid #333;
}

.notice {
  color: #859900;
  font-style: italic;
}

.line {
  line-height: 1.8rem;
  display: table;
}

#logs {
  margin-top: 1.5rem;
  padding: 1.5rem;
}
</style></head><body><h1>IRC channel logs</h1><h2>2024-11-30.log</h2><p><a href="/bootstrappable">back to list of logs</a></p><div id="logs"><div class="line" id="020249"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#020249" label="[02:02:49]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">oriansj: there's also the issue of applications only being certified (by the government, etc.) on one specific platform - this was largely what kept Itanium alive in the banking industry.</span></div><div class="line" id="021252"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#021252" label="[02:12:52]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Googulator: if you are referring to FFIEC, that didn't require certification of the software (let alone the platform the software was running on) but certification of processes (which could have been done as part of the annual audit). But poorly thought out support contracts that the banking industry agreed to which locked them into a single vendor for over a decade for &quot;reduced costs&quot; which ended up costing them much more.</span></div><div class="line" id="021402"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#021402" label="[02:14:02]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Now there are manufactoring environments where multi-million dollar machines are attached to a nonstop server that can't be replaced</span></div><div class="line" id="021435"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#021435" label="[02:14:35]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and the FDA didn't certify any nonstop systems (oddly a bunch of windows 95 systems did though)</span></div><div class="line" id="021850"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#021850" label="[02:18:50]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">oriansj: by the way, any chance you could review <a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/oriansj/bootstrap-seeds/pull/46">https://github.com/oriansj/bootstrap-seeds/pull/46</a>  ?</span></div><div class="line" id="023901"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#023901" label="[02:39:01]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">stikonas: of course</span></div><div class="line" id="024613"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#024613" label="[02:46:13]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">hmmm, odd that no one picked up that all of the ecx/ebx/ebp instructions could be just converted to cx/bx/bp and you could save a good few bytes</span></div><div class="line" id="024910"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#024910" label="[02:49:10]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">(hint E5 04 vs C1E5 04 sort of thing)</span></div><div class="line" id="025825"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#025825" label="[02:58:25]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">(although admittedly hex0 is kind of running out of instructions to possibly optimize)</span></div><div class="line" id="025845"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#025845" label="[02:58:45]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">If only there was a COM like executable format for Linux/BSD/etc</span></div><div class="line" id="031057"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#031057" label="[03:10:57]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and as hex0 in the seeds is only expected to get exactly 2 arguments (input file and output file) it is ok if it breaks if one passes more arguments</span></div><div class="line" id="034014"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#034014" label="[03:40:14]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">(although it might just me seeing a RASM bug)</span></div><div class="line" id="064417"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#064417" label="[06:44:17]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">seems rebasing the redhat nptl patchset for linux-2.4.21 onto linux-2.4.37.11 isn't too easy</span></div><div class="line" id="064518"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#064518" label="[06:45:18]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i'll give it a weekend, in worst case i may rollback kernel to some linux-2.4.2x that may have nptl support cleanly applied easier</span></div><div class="line" id="114449"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#114449" label="[11:44:49]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">oriansj: I thought we want to keep it working with more arguments...</span></div><div class="line" id="114510"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#114510" label="[11:45:10]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">it is possible that something might be optimized if assume argc = 3...</span></div><div class="line" id="114533"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#114533" label="[11:45:33]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i.e. by not having to write 3 to some register and instead doing xchg</span></div><div class="line" id="114541"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#114541" label="[11:45:41]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but this feels a bit confusing</span></div><div class="line" id="120716"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#120716" label="[12:07:16]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">stikonas: fair enough</span></div><div class="line" id="120850"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#120850" label="[12:08:50]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and I do have concerns about if Linux gets more strict about ELF again and breaking hex0 again.</span></div><div class="line" id="121250"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#121250" label="[12:12:50]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">if we wanted to get crazy with optimization, we could assume only one use of hex0-seed (which is to build a more proper hex0)</span></div><div class="line" id="122246"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#122246" label="[12:22:46]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">well, so far I think we should be standard compliant</span></div><div class="line" id="122310"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#122310" label="[12:23:10]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">there are some attemts long time ago at really small binaries (far smaller than hex) that print something</span></div><div class="line" id="122323"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#122323" label="[12:23:23]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but I think those assumed some implementation specific details on Linux 2.2 or so</span></div><div class="line" id="122621"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#122621" label="[12:26:21]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">oriansj: by the way, where is that ecx-&gt;cx thing that you mentioned?</span></div><div class="line" id="122649"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#122649" label="[12:26:49]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">looking at <a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/oriansj/stage0-posix-x86/blob/a15adc72f099506fb0d40c8824d7aaa97735f7c3/GAS/hex0_x86.S">https://github.com/oriansj/stage0-posix-x86/blob/a15adc72f099506fb0d40c8824d7aaa97735f7c3/GAS/hex0_x86.S</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="122658"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#122658" label="[12:26:58]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I think we only use full register where we have to</span></div><div class="line" id="122707"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#122707" label="[12:27:07]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">e.g. store 32-bit memory addresses</span></div><div class="line" id="122713"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#122713" label="[12:27:13]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">or file descriptors</span></div><div class="line" id="123137"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#123137" label="[12:31:37]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">though probably there are cases in kaem...</span></div><div class="line" id="153711"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#153711" label="[15:37:11]">&lt;unmush&gt;</a></span><span class="message">my mono patch series against guix is now available at <a rel="nofollow" href="https://debbugs.gnu.org/cgi/bugreport.cgi?bug=74609">https://debbugs.gnu.org/cgi/bugreport.cgi?bug=74609</a>  if anybody is interested</span></div><div class="line" id="161817"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#161817" label="[16:18:17]">&lt;lilyp&gt;</a></span><span class="message">unmush: would you like doing this with `git send-email'?</span></div><div class="line" id="171500"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#171500" label="[17:15:00]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">the linux-2.x is confusing</span></div><div class="line" id="171555"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#171555" label="[17:15:55]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and it is relevant since bootstrappable relies upon it with fiwix, with the efforts that one was re-implemented from scratch</span></div><div class="line" id="171631"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#171631" label="[17:16:31]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yet, looking at the kernel version history and branches of linux-2.4 itself</span></div><div class="line" id="171703"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#171703" label="[17:17:03]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">redhat9 and fedora, and too centos all of them abandoned the linux-2.4 series before it reached EOL</span></div><div class="line" id="171724"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#171724" label="[17:17:24]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">important patches were not mainlined back to linux-2.4.37.11</span></div><div class="line" id="171751"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#171751" label="[17:17:51]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">bellard too quit at linux-2.4.2x</span></div><div class="line" id="171827"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#171827" label="[17:18:27]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">then, in parallel, for ~10years, 2.6.x and 2.4.x were maintained, and reached EOL at about the same time</span></div><div class="line" id="171907"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#171907" label="[17:19:07]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but redhat/fedora/ibm did not bother to rebase important(!) patches for nptl back onto 2.4.x</span></div><div class="line" id="171920"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#171920" label="[17:19:20]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and no other distribution did to my knowledge</span></div><div class="line" id="171947"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#171947" label="[17:19:47]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">however, 2.4 series was the last known which could be supported by tcc/tccboot too</span></div><div class="line" id="172017"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#172017" label="[17:20:17]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">that's one incentive, to pick up maintenance of it again, 10 years later</span></div><div class="line" id="172038"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#172038" label="[17:20:38]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">another one is, 2.6 series increased lines of code excessively</span></div><div class="line" id="172106"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#172106" label="[17:21:06]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">then back to fiwix, considered linux-2.x ABI that relevant, it was re-implemented from scratch, without nptl again</span></div><div class="line" id="172204"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#172204" label="[17:22:04]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">centos which i tried first, did not ship nptl/o1 scheduler patchset inside their SRPM, although their kernel could not have compiled without those at least</span></div><div class="line" id="172232"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#172232" label="[17:22:32]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">legacy.redhat.com kept those, gladly, and too fedora got some srpm archive which contain them</span></div><div class="line" id="172304"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#172304" label="[17:23:04]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and it is two big changsets to rebase onto 2.4.37, the one for o1 scheduler, and nptl</span></div><div class="line" id="172420"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#172420" label="[17:24:20]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">nptl support from kernel seems preferrable</span></div><div class="line" id="172507"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#172507" label="[17:25:07]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i think it is feasible, although it takes a little while longer than expected, to re-unite a tcc-compiled kernel-2.4 with a musl-libc userspace</span></div><div class="line" id="172612"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#172612" label="[17:26:12]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i am not sure yet, if i'll stick to some 2.4.2x or 2.4.3x series, since redhat terminated at 2.4.2x</span></div><div class="line" id="175453"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#175453" label="[17:54:53]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;cosinusoidally&gt; I suppose it depends what your aims are. I've been playing around with some old 2.4 (and earlier) bases slackware VMs and they are surprisingly usable, and complete, desktop systems.</span></div><div class="line" id="175507"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#175507" label="[17:55:07]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;cosinusoidally&gt; * based</span></div><div class="line" id="175645"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#175645" label="[17:56:45]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">at first glance, linux-2.4 kernel is far less lines of code, fiwix is even less with some absent usb/smp/hardware support</span></div><div class="line" id="175726"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#175726" label="[17:57:26]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">linux-2.4 is hitting the sweet spot in between this</span></div><div class="line" id="175741"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#175741" label="[17:57:41]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">next, it can be fully supported with tcc compiler</span></div><div class="line" id="175819"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#175819" label="[17:58:19]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">it is missing nptl only for compatibility with musl-libc, however, nptl is available</span></div><div class="line" id="175845"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#175845" label="[17:58:45]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">then, it is possible, i think, to spawn most recent software versions atop linux-2.4 kernel</span></div><div class="line" id="175907"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#175907" label="[17:59:07]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">including threading</span></div><div class="line" id="175928"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#175928" label="[17:59:28]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">furthermore, linux-2.4 can be hooked into the &quot;kernel bootstrapping&quot; dependency chain</span></div><div class="line" id="180015"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#180015" label="[18:00:15]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and, there is interesting extensions for linux-2.4 which later kernels haven't got, such as systrace from nils provos</span></div><div class="line" id="180102"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#180102" label="[18:01:02]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">as far as security/forensics were concerned</span></div><div class="line" id="180128"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#180128" label="[18:01:28]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">since this is x86/32bit, i may have to backport nilfs2 filesystem too</span></div><div class="line" id="180340"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#180340" label="[18:03:40]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">another problem is, Intel began phasing out UEFI/CSM that is needed, otherwise the idea is a long-term stable system could be booted almost everywhere</span></div><div class="line" id="180344"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#180344" label="[18:03:44]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">without updates necessary</span></div><div class="line" id="180856"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#180856" label="[18:08:56]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i've wasted a whole night with rebasing centos patchsets onto linux-2.4.37.11 just to find out their linux srpm was incomplete</span></div><div class="line" id="180948"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#180948" label="[18:09:48]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">the redhat9/fedora-1.0 srpm seem complete, so i'll use that</span></div><div class="line" id="181049"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#181049" label="[18:10:49]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">since bootstrappable had picked some LTS linux-kernel from the 4.x series, i suspect a 2.x wasn't a bad choice either</span></div><div class="line" id="181140"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#181140" label="[18:11:40]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">otherwise, i am not interested in desktop stuff</span></div><div class="line" id="181156"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#181156" label="[18:11:56]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">that's all what i consider optional</span></div><div class="line" id="181245"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#181245" label="[18:12:45]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i think i can finish rebasing the nptl patchset this weekend, to see how many of those ~500ebuilds that i got for support with tcc might work</span></div><div class="line" id="181457"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#181457" label="[18:14:57]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">*nilfs2 got 64bit timestamps which are needed for year2038, and linux-2.4 hasn't got any filesystem ready on board for 32bit POSIX</span></div><div class="line" id="181522"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#181522" label="[18:15:22]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">64bit time_t itself is another breakage inside linux, which got addressed rather late</span></div><div class="line" id="181544"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#181544" label="[18:15:44]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i think the kernel version that bootstrappable had chosen wasn't year2038 ready with 32bit</span></div><div class="line" id="181703"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#181703" label="[18:17:03]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">officially, linux was advertizing removal of their BKL, but i think it was absent nptl support which was a hindrance to scalability with linux-2.4</span></div><div class="line" id="181741"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#181741" label="[18:17:41]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">so, well, that's another incentive, if it was scalable</span></div><div class="line" id="204058"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#204058" label="[20:40:58]">&lt;unmush&gt;</a></span><span class="message">lilyp: AFAIK I can't with protonmail unless I first set up some bridge thing</span></div><div class="line" id="204533"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#204533" label="[20:45:33]">&lt;lilyp&gt;</a></span><span class="message">weird… it's unencrypted mail anyway, so you should just be able to use whatever smtp client you have</span></div><div class="line" id="204918"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#204918" label="[20:49:18]">&lt;unmush&gt;</a></span><span class="message">AFAIK protonmail is webmail-only for free accounts</span></div><div class="line" id="205115"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#205115" label="[20:51:15]">&lt;unmush&gt;</a></span><span class="message">note that the same patch series (the output of 'git format-patch ...') is available as a .tar.gz at <a rel="nofollow" href="https://unmush.de1.hashbang.sh/misc-files/mono-series.tar.gz">https://unmush.de1.hashbang.sh/misc-files/mono-series.tar.gz</a> </span></div><br /></div></body></html>